WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.443
Hi, I'm Mizzen!

00:00:02.443 --> 00:00:05.134
MiniMax opened the weights of M3.1 Flash.

00:00:05.134 --> 00:00:06.672
The name sounds small.

00:00:06.672 --> 00:00:08.210
The files say otherwise.

00:00:09.683 --> 00:00:11.999
Flash keeps the whole backbone of M3.

00:00:11.999 --> 00:00:14.976
What changes is how much memory each token reads.

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:19.998
A token climbs sixty layers.

00:00:19.998 --> 00:00:21.579
The first three are dense.

00:00:21.579 --> 00:00:25.373
The other fifty seven each hold a hundred and twenty eight experts.

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:29.426
A router picks four experts per layer, so about twenty three billion

00:00:29.426 --> 00:00:32.756
of four hundred and twenty seven billion parameters do the work.

00:00:33.176 --> 00:00:35.718
The experts now ship as four bit numbers.

00:00:35.718 --> 00:00:39.213
The checkpoint drops from about eight hundred and seventy gigabytes to

00:00:39.213 --> 00:00:40.484
two hundred and fifty.

00:00:40.904 --> 00:00:44.519
A small draft head guesses seven tokens ahead, and the full model

00:00:44.519 --> 00:00:46.929
checks them, so the output does not change.

00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:52.170
In full attention, every new token reads every past key and value.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.985
At a million tokens, that is the slow part.

00:00:55.405 --> 00:00:59.126
Sparse attention scores past blocks with a tiny index and attends exactly

00:00:59.126 --> 00:01:01.607
to the best sixteen blocks per head group.

00:01:02.027 --> 00:01:04.577
M3 kept three layers on full attention.

00:01:04.577 --> 00:01:06.399
Flash makes all sixty sparse.

00:01:08.217 --> 00:01:12.327
At a million tokens, plain attention reads about a hundred and twenty

00:01:12.327 --> 00:01:13.697
nine gigabytes per token.

00:01:13.697 --> 00:01:17.465
Flash reads about sixteen, but still stores every key and value.

00:01:19.175 --> 00:01:22.450
MiniMax's own table has Flash leading three of fifteen rows.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:26.053
The newest DeepSeek Flash leads four of the six they share.

00:01:27.558 --> 00:01:29.528
So Flash is not a smaller model.

00:01:29.528 --> 00:01:32.905
It is the same model, made to read fewer bytes per token.

00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:36.242
Same backbone.

00:01:36.242 --> 00:01:37.381
Sparse attention everywhere.

00:01:37.381 --> 00:01:40.038
Four bit experts and a draft head.

00:01:40.458 --> 00:01:42.524
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:42.524 --> 00:01:43.114
I'm Mizzen.

00:01:43.114 --> 00:01:43.409
Bye!
